+---
+id: task-004-support-customer-charged-shipping-on-an-order-whose-goods-totalled-exactly-fifty-euros
+type: task
+title: "Support: customer charged shipping on an order whose goods totalled exactly fifty euros"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Report
+
+Support reported a customer paid €4.90 for delivery on an order whose goods came to exactly €50.00,
+although the site promises free delivery from that amount.
+
+## Investigation
+
+This is exactly the off-by-one free-shipping boundary bug already fixed on `main` in
+`src/shipping.ts` (`shippingCost`): it used to read `if (goods > FREE_SHIPPING_FROM_CENTS) return 0;`,
+which required the goods to be *strictly more than* €50.00, charging shipping on an order of exactly
+€50.00 — matching this complaint exactly. It now reads `if (goods >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM_CENTS) return
+0;`, matching README.md's "Shipping is free when the goods come to €50.00 or more". A test in
+`test/orders.test.ts` ("ships free when the goods come to exactly the free-shipping amount") covers a
+single-line order of exactly 5000 cents. I additionally checked a two-line order (3000 + 2000 cents,
+no discounts) summing to exactly 5000 cents of goods: shippingCents is 0 on current `main`.
+
+`npm test` passes (20/20). No code change was needed.
+
+## Outcome
+
+No bug on current `main`. Reply to support: an order whose goods total exactly €50.00 now ships free;
+the customer was likely charged on an earlier version of the service. If she has not been refunded the
+€4.90, that is a one-off refund for support to process, not a code fix.
